   135	<aside class="three-things" data-layer="fact">
   136	<p class="tt-label">Three things to know</p>
   137	<ol>
   138	<li>Judge Christopher Cooper denied Representative Joyce Beatty's request for an emergency hearing on September 17. In the same minute order, he required written notice more than 30 days before the defendants implement a change to the project plans previously presented to the court, including demolition of the main building.<sup class="src-ref">
   139	<a href="#src-21" data-record-href="/records/upi-kennedy-center-2026-09-17/" data-passage="more than 30 days before the implementation of any changes to the scope of the project plans presented to the Court" aria-label="Source 21: read the evidence">21</a>
   140	</sup>
   141	</li>
   142	<li>The reproduced sentence does not say that every physical change is covered or state whether demolition would be lawful. It makes notice turn on changes to the scope of the project plans previously presented to the court.<sup class="src-ref">
   143	<a href="#src-21" data-record-href="/records/upi-kennedy-center-2026-09-17/" data-passage="changes to the scope of the project plans presented to the Court" aria-label="Source 21: read the evidence">21</a>
   144	</sup>
   145	</li>
   146	<li>The executive director said the immediate closure was a seven-day safety measure unless extended. The defendants characterized it as separate from the board's renovation decision.<sup class="src-ref">
   147	<a href="#src-5" data-record-href="/records/floca-declaration-86-1-2026-09-17/" data-passage="which will last for seven days, unless extended" aria-label="Source 5: read the evidence">5</a>
   148	</sup>
   149	<sup class="src-ref">
   150	<a href="#src-4" data-record-href="/records/defendants-response-86-2026-09-17/" data-passage="separate from any Board decision related to closure for ongoing renovations" aria-label="Source 4: read the evidence">4</a>
   151	</sup>
   152	</li>
   153	</ol>
   154	</aside>
   155	<p class="railed rail-note" data-layer="fact">The federal docket's September 17 minute order was not available as a court-hosted document in the public sources we could preserve. UPI reproduces the operative sentence below in full. Beatty's counsel quotes part of it and describes the requirement as 30 days' advance warning; the surrounding motions, declarations, earlier opinions and board records are preserved separately.<sup class="src-ref">
   156	<a href="#src-21" data-record-href="/records/upi-kennedy-center-2026-09-17/" data-passage="To avoid any confusion on that score given more recent events" aria-label="Source 21: read the evidence">21</a>
   157	</sup>
   158	<sup class="src-ref">
   159	<a href="#src-18" data-record-href="/records/beatty-counsel-reaction-2026-09-17/" data-passage="any changes to the scope of the project plans" aria-label="Source 18: read the evidence" data-passages="30 days’ advance warning">18</a>
   160	</sup>
   161	</p>
   162	<div data-layer="proof" class="event-record-context">
   163	<p class="sources-line">This story rests on filings in Beatty v. Trump and D.C. Preservation League v. Trump; the court's May 29 opinions; the board's August meeting record; the August 28 joint report; the September 16 scheduling order; federal statutes governing the Kennedy Center; the September 17 minute order as reproduced in full by UPI and quoted in part by Beatty's counsel; and the coverage records checked against those documents.</p>
   164	</div>
   165	<section id="what-happened">
   166	<p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The request and the ruling</p>
   167	<h2 data-layer="fact">The judge refused the hearing and added an advance-notice rule</h2>
   168	<p data-layer="narrative">Beatty asked for an emergency hearing after the main building closed and after the board voted to close it for renovation. The defendants answered that the immediate closure was separate from the renovation decision. Executive Director Matt Floca described it as a temporary safety measure and declared that structural deterioration created acute risks to public safety. He said the initial closure would last seven days unless extended.<sup class="src-ref">
   169	<a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/beatty-motion-84-2026-09-16/" data-passage="MOTION FOR EMERGENCY HEARING" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a>
   170	</sup>
   171	<sup class="src-ref">
   172	<a href="#src-4" data-record-href="/records/defendants-response-86-2026-09-17/" data-passage="separate from any Board decision related to closure for ongoing renovations" aria-label="Source 4: read the evidence">4</a>
   173	</sup>
   174	<sup class="src-ref">
   175	<a href="#src-5" data-record-href="/records/floca-declaration-86-1-2026-09-17/" data-passage="acute risks to public safety resulting from continued structural deterioration" aria-label="Source 5: read the evidence" data-passages="which will last for seven days, unless extended">5</a>
   176	</sup>
   177	</p>
   178	<p data-layer="narrative">The filing also put two public signals before Cooper. Trump had told reporters that without recognition for his administration's role in the renovation and fundraising, the building would close and end up being ripped down. The filing included a photograph of him reading a placard that appeared to say “Kennedy Center DEMOLISHED.” ABC reported that the published images did not show the entire placard and that the visible word was cut off at “DEMOLIS.” A Kennedy Center spokesperson separately said that nothing was being demolished.<sup class="src-ref">
   179	<a href="#src-3" data-record-href="/records/beatty-notice-85-2026-09-17/" aria-label="Source 3: read the evidence" data-passages="appears to read “Kennedy Center DEMOLISHED.”">3</a>
   180	</sup>
   181	<sup class="src-ref">
   182	<a href="#src-19" data-record-href="/records/abc-kennedy-center-2026-09-17/" data-passage="The published images don't show the entire placard" aria-label="Source 19: read the evidence" data-passages="cut off as “DEMOLIS.”">19</a>
   183	</sup>
   184	<sup class="src-ref">
   185	<a href="#src-26" data-record-href="/records/cbs-kennedy-center-2026-09-17/" data-passage="nothing is being demolished" aria-label="Source 26: read the evidence">26</a>
   186	</sup>
   187	</p>
   188	<p data-layer="narrative">Cooper denied the hearing. He nevertheless ordered the defendants to give plaintiffs in both cases written notice before implementing a covered change.<sup class="src-ref">
   189	<a href="#src-21" data-record-href="/records/upi-kennedy-center-2026-09-17/" data-passage="Beatty's request for a hearing was denied" aria-label="Source 21: read the evidence" data-passages="provide such notice in writing to Plaintiffs in both cases">21</a>
   190	</sup>
   191	</p>
   192	<figure class="quote-card" data-layer="fact">
   193	<figcaption class="qc-head">
   194	<span class="qc-speaker">U.S. District Judge Christopher Cooper</span>
   195	<span class="qc-sep">&middot;</span>
   196	<span class="qc-when">September 17, 2026</span>
   197	<span class="qc-sep">&middot;</span>
   198	<span class="qc-where">Minute order, as reproduced by UPI</span>
   199	</figcaption>
   200	<blockquote class="qc-words">
   201	<p>To avoid any confusion on that score given more recent events, the Court hereby ORDERS Defendants [the Kennedy Center board] to provide such notice in writing to Plaintiffs in both cases more than 30 days before the implementation of any changes to the scope of the project plans presented to the Court in D.C. Preservation League [a separate case], including but not limited to any 'demolition' of the Center's main building.<sup class="src-ref">
   202	<a href="#src-21" data-record-href="/records/upi-kennedy-center-2026-09-17/" data-passage="To avoid any confusion on that score given more recent events" aria-label="Source 21: read the evidence">21</a>
   203	</sup>
   204	</p>
   205	</blockquote>
   206	</figure>
   207	<p data-layer="narrative">The order also required the administration to update Cooper by September 23 on the status of the temporary closure and emergency repairs.<sup class="src-ref">
   208	<a href="#src-19" data-record-href="/records/abc-kennedy-center-2026-09-17/" data-passage="update him on the status of the" aria-label="Source 19: read the evidence" data-passages="emergency repairs">19</a>
   209	</sup> Beatty's counsel said Cooper “ordered discovery into the Board’s actions related to a potential demolition.”<sup class="src-ref">
   210	<a href="#src-18" data-record-href="/records/beatty-counsel-reaction-2026-09-17/" data-passage="ordered discovery into the Board’s actions related to a potential demolition" aria-label="Source 18: read the evidence">18</a>
   211	</sup> UPI's reproduced order text contains the notice command but no discovery provision.<sup class="src-ref">
   212	<a href="#src-21" data-record-href="/records/upi-kennedy-center-2026-09-17/" data-passage="the Court hereby ORDERS Defendants" aria-label="Source 21: read the evidence">21</a>
   213	</sup>
   214	</p>
   215	</section>
   216	<section id="the-catch">
   217	<aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
   218	<p class="tt-label">The catch</p>
   219	<ol>
   220	<li>
   221	<h3>The period is more than 30 days.</h3>The operative sentence says notice must arrive more than 30 days before implementation. “30 days” is a useful shorthand, but it drops the word that sets the boundary.<sup class="src-ref">
   222	<a href="#src-21" data-record-href="/records/upi-kennedy-center-2026-09-17/" data-passage="more than 30 days before the implementation" aria-label="Source 21: read the evidence">21</a>
   223	</sup>
   224	</li>
   225	<li>
   226	<h3>The order follows the plans already put before the court.</h3>It does not say “any major physical changes” require notice. That phrase comes from the AP headline. The trigger in the reproduced order is a change to the scope of the project plans presented in the preservation case, with demolition named as an example.<sup class="src-ref">
   227	<a href="#src-20" data-record-href="/records/pbs-ap-kennedy-center-2026-09-17/" data-passage="any major physical changes" aria-label="Source 20: read the evidence">20</a>
   228	</sup>
   229	<sup class="src-ref">
   230	<a href="#src-21" data-record-href="/records/upi-kennedy-center-2026-09-17/" data-passage="changes to the scope of the project plans presented to the Court" aria-label="Source 21: read the evidence">21</a>
   231	</sup>
   232	</li>
   233	<li>
   234	<h3>Notice is not a final ruling on demolition.</h3>The reproduced sentence imposes an advance-notice requirement. It neither approves nor permanently bars demolition.<sup class="src-ref">
   235	<a href="#src-21" data-record-href="/records/upi-kennedy-center-2026-09-17/" data-passage="more than 30 days before the implementation" aria-label="Source 21: read the evidence">21</a>
   236	</sup>
   237	</li>
   238	</ol>
   239	</aside>
   240	</section>
   241	<section id="where-this-sits">
   242	<p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The earlier record</p>
   243	<h2 data-layer="fact">The court had relied on a no-demolition position</h2>
   244	<p data-layer="narrative">In May, Cooper denied preliminary relief in the preservation case after considering sworn statements about the project. The opinion said the building's footprint and overall shape and size seemed unlikely to change permanently, and it described the anticipated open-space changes as relatively limited. The court said those findings were tentative and could change if the facts changed.<sup class="src-ref">
   245	<a href="#src-9" data-record-href="/records/preservation-opinion-45-2026-05-29/" data-passage="footprint and overall shape and size seem unlikely to change in a permanent manner" aria-label="Source 9: read the evidence" data-passages="these relatively limited alterations&#10;tentative">9</a>
   246	</sup>
   247	</p>
   248	<p data-layer="narrative">On August 24, the Justice Department argued that without the administration's work and recognition, the Center would deteriorate until it was “required to be taken down.” The filing said some people had proposed replacing it with a large outdoor amphitheater overlooking the Potomac River. That was a conditional litigation position, not an approved demolition project.<sup class="src-ref">
   249	<a href="#src-31" data-record-href="/records/defendants-response-recognition-68-2026-08-24/" data-passage="will be required to be taken down" aria-label="Source 31: read the evidence" data-passages="a large outdoor amphitheater overlooking the Potomac River">31</a>
   250	</sup>
   251	</p>
   252	<p data-layer="narrative">The parties' August 28 joint report made the relevant promise explicit: the defendants would inform the plaintiffs and the court if the board's plans reversed the position that the building would not be demolished and no new structure would be built.<sup class="src-ref">
   253	<a href="#src-7" data-record-href="/records/preservation-joint-report-62-2026-08-28/" data-passage="Defendants have pledged to inform Plaintiffs and the Court" aria-label="Source 7: read the evidence" data-passages="building will not be demolished and that no new structure will be constructed">7</a>
   254	</sup>
   255	</p>
   256	<p data-layer="narrative">Congress appropriated about $257 million for capital repair, restoration, the maintenance backlog and security structures, with the money available through September 30, 2029.<sup class="src-ref">
   257	<a href="#src-30" data-record-href="/records/public-law-119-21-kennedy-center/" data-passage="$256,657,000, to remain" aria-label="Source 30: read the evidence" data-passages="available until September 30, 2029">30</a>
   258	</sup>
   259	</p>
   260	<p data-layer="narrative">The August minutes say that keeping the main building partly open would leave the appropriation covering only about half of the work, with a $124 million shortfall after utilities. The minutes put the closure estimate at about $285 million.<sup class="src-ref">

