   Record bytes: “Plaintiff may seek discovery regarding the subject of their 85 Notice of Supplemental Authority” in the [CourtListener minute-order screenshot](https://am11.mediaite.com/med/cnt/uploads/2026/09/Screenshot-2026-09-17-at-2-08-21-PM-1200x336.jpg).  
   Correct account: Cooper did not order unrestricted discovery, as counsel claimed. He expressly allowed Beatty to seek discovery about the demolition evidence, subject to the federal rules and scheduling order. Omitting this sentence materially understates the ruling.

2. **Major: The September 13 trustee packet tied recognition of Trump to a claimed financial rescue.**  
   Page bytes: “The declaration dates the packet to the closure deliberations but does not reproduce its contents or identify the recognition proposal.” [Article source](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-sol/src/pages/events/kennedy-center/2026-09-17-30-day-notice-before-demolition.astro:131)  
   Record bytes: The distributed draft said the Center “will not be able to support its payroll obligations” and that “without such appropriate recognition it is unlikely” Trump would lead its fiscal rescue. [The Washington Post reproduced the resolutions](https://www.washingtonpost.com/style/2026/09/14/read-text-proposal-close-kennedy-center/).  
   Correct account: The packet contained a closure resolution and a separate draft recognition resolution with ten facade options. The latter tied recognition to Trump’s fundraising help and claimed fiscal collapse within weeks. Reporting indicates that proposal was dropped from the meeting agenda, not adopted. The page had enough information to distinguish the distributed proposal from the action actually taken.

3. **Major: The page lacks the newest independent financial baseline.**  
   Page bytes: “The August minutes say that keeping the main building partly open would leave the appropriation covering only about half of the work.” [Article source](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-sol/src/pages/events/kennedy-center/2026-09-17-30-day-notice-before-demolition.astro:91)  
   Record bytes: The newly obtained Form 990 reported “more than $516 million in total revenue,” a “$48 million ‘bad debt expense,’” and program-service revenue falling from about $105 million to $89 million. [The Atlantic’s filing analysis](https://www.theatlantic.com/culture/2026/09/kennedy-center-trump-tax-return/688669/).  
   Correct account: The financial crisis is real but not cleanly explained by the board’s shortfall comparisons. The filing spans months under both prior and current leadership, contains a large one-time federal appropriation, and cannot by itself establish a $47 million operating deficit. It also says the annual audit had not been completed. This materially complicates the recognition, rescue, and closure rationale.

4. **Moderate: Current layoffs and the CFO’s resignation are absent.**  
   Page bytes: “The May opinion records Floca's estimate that between 75 and 175 of roughly 300 employees would be affected.” [Article source](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-sol/src/pages/events/kennedy-center/2026-09-17-30-day-notice-before-demolition.astro:99)  
   Record bytes: The Center “laid off more than a dozen employees Friday,” while its “chief financial officer ... resigned.” [The Washington Post](https://www.washingtonpost.com/style/2026/09/18/fresh-layoffs-begin-kennedy-center-closure-continues/) identified 14 cuts across IT, communications, marketing, and sales.  
   Correct account: The human impact was no longer merely a May estimate. Reported September 18 consequences included current layoffs, senior staff departures, and the CFO’s resignation.

5. **Moderate: The underlying staff email was not preserved or cited directly.**  
   Page bytes: “The defendants characterized it as separate from the board's renovation decision.” [Article source](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-sol/src/pages/events/kennedy-center/2026-09-17-30-day-notice-before-demolition.astro:51)  
   Record bytes: Floca’s email said closure “will allow operations staff to determine whether any other severe safety incident is imminent,” while also saying it was “not a decision to effectuate the Board's separate vote.” [ECF 86-2](https://storage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.dcd.287972/gov.uscourts.dcd.287972.86.2.pdf)  
   Correct account: The direct exhibit supports management’s separation claim but also confirms that the full-building closure preceded completion of the imminent-risk assessment. Both parts belong in the account.

6. **Minor: One evidence link is malformed in the built page.**  
   Page bytes: `data-passages="shortfall of <a href="#src-13"...24M`. [Built page](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-sol/dist/events/kennedy-center/2026-09-17-30-day-notice-before-demolition/index.html:3)  
   Record bytes: “insufficient funds (shortfall of $124M)” in the [August board minutes](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-sol/data/sources/kennedy-center/board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13.txt:386).  
   Correct account: The prose number is right, but the generated citation markup corrupts the recorded passage and can break provenance inspection.

## Is There More to the Story?

Yes.

- **Backward search:** The no-demolition representation, May injunction, August closure vote, recognition litigation, JLL plan, Delta work, and September 13 packet show that demolition anxiety emerged inside a longer dispute over closure, naming, fundraising, and board governance.
- **Forward search:** The September 18 motions, layoffs, CFO resignation, protest, Trump’s non-answer to a direct demolition question, and the newly reported Form 990 materially extend the story.
- **Horizontal search:** Workforce effects, nonprofit finances, preservation oversight, building-safety evidence, and trustee information flows reveal consequences and motives outside the page’s court-order frame.
- **Omission classes:** procedural scope, causal context, affected parties, financial baseline, source-chain completeness, and later consequences.

No accessible record establishes an approved demolition plan. That does not cure the omissions above.

## Completeness Verdict

**Bounded and substantially incomplete.**

The core notice-rule explanation is mostly accurate, but the omitted discovery language changes the legal result. The missing trustee packet, financial baseline, and current workforce consequences prevent a complete account of why the closure happened and what followed.

The audit is bounded because several central originals remain inaccessible: the docket-native minute entry, the final Delta report and appendices, Exhibit B’s complete trustee packet, Exhibit C’s preliminary minutes, the latest Form 990 itself, and the unfinished annual audit.

## Critical and Major Findings

- **Critical:** None established.
- **Major:** The discovery clause is omitted and effectively contradicted.
- **Major:** The recognition-for-rescue proposal in the trustee packet is missing.
- **Major:** The newest financial filing and its substantial cautions are missing.
- **Moderate:** Current layoffs, executive departure, and the direct staff email are missing.
- **Minor:** The $124 million evidence link is malformed.

The first three defects can change a reader’s understanding of the ruling, the board’s motivations, and the claimed financial necessity.

## Before, After, and Around the Story

- **Before:** The court’s May injunction blocked the earlier closure. The board voted again in August, then reaffirmed closure on September 15 after receiving Delta materials and reports of soffit corrosion and fallen plaster.
- **Immediate event:** Management closed the main building on September 16 for an initial seven days. Cooper’s September 17 order denied an additional emergency hearing, required a sworn status report, imposed more than 30 days’ notice for covered project-scope changes, and allowed discovery concerning the demolition evidence.
- **After:** Defendants sought dissolution of the injunction and partial summary judgment on September 18. At least 14 layoffs were reported that day, the CFO had resigned, and thousands protested outside the Center.
- **Around:** The trustee materials connected physical closure, claimed imminent insolvency, Trump recognition, and fundraising. The latest tax filing complicates both the inherited-crisis narrative and any simple claim that current management caused the entire shortfall.

## Accuracy and Verification

The page accurately states that:

- Notice must arrive **more than** 30 days before implementation.
- The trigger is a change to the project scope previously presented to the court.
- The order does not adjudicate whether demolition would be lawful.
- The seven-day closure and the board’s renovation closure were presented by defendants as separate decisions.
- No accessible filing establishes an approved demolition plan.
- Trump’s intent remained unresolved on September 18.

The discovery treatment is inaccurate by omission.

Video review covered 307 one-second frames:

- The [Acyn tarmac video](https://x.com/Acyn/status/2100354290858750212): 94 frames plus its complete transcript. It supports the “ripped down” quotation.
- The [CBS package](https://www.cbsnews.com/news/kennedy-center-demolished-trump-photos/): 202 frames plus its complete transcript. It preserves both the spokesperson’s “nothing is being demolished” denial and Trump’s contrary conditional statement.
- The [Howard Lutnick ceiling footage](https://x.com/howardlutnick/status/2099643013894939087): 11 frames. It is silent and visually shows plaster falling and debris afterward, but does not independently prove the date, cause, dimensions, or building-wide danger.
- The Aaron Rupar placard item is a still photograph, not a video. The published image remains cropped at the relevant word.

None of these visual records shows an approved demolition plan or active demolition of the main building.

## Source and Corroboration Problems

- **Notice order:** One underlying judicial record. UPI, AP, ABC, USA Today, CBS, Mediaite, and counsel’s release are carriers, not seven independent factual lineages.
- **Safety closure:** Floca’s declaration and staff email form one management lineage. The silent ceiling footage independently supports that a plaster fall occurred, but the engineering analyses and complete Delta report were not available for independent review.
- **Demolition intent:** The tarmac statement and placard photograph are two public signals. The Kennedy Center’s denial is a separate institutional statement. None resolves intent or proves an approved plan.
- **Trustee packet:** One underlying packet, referenced in a declaration and reproduced in part by press reporting. The complete filed exhibit was unavailable.
- **Layoffs:** One Washington Post reporting lineage based on three unnamed sources. It is material but not yet supported by an official Center record.
- **Financial filing:** The Atlantic reviewed the new Form 990, but the filing itself was not publicly retrievable during this audit.
- **Page inventory:** Thirty-five records are listed, but domain count overstates independence and ECF 86-2 was omitted despite being publicly downloadable.

## Reconstructed Story

**Established**

Cooper denied Beatty’s request for an additional emergency hearing. He required a detailed September 23 status report, reaffirmed the defendants’ duty to report reversal of their no-demolition position, required more than 30 days’ written notice before covered project-scope changes, and allowed discovery concerning Beatty’s demolition evidence.

The order did not approve demolition, permanently forbid it, or find that an approved demolition plan existed.

Management described the September 16 closure as an initial seven-day safety measure distinct from the board’s renovation closure. The board’s longer closure remained subject to the injunction.

**Strongly supported**

A substantial piece of foyer plaster fell on September 4. Engineering work identified serious soffit-panel corrosion. The building has significant repair needs.

The Center was experiencing financial and operational stress, although its precise causes, size, and allocation between operating and capital accounts remain unresolved.

**Disputed**

Whether the immediate closure was an independent safety response or a way to effectuate the enjoined renovation closure.

Whether the board had adequate and balanced information when it reaffirmed closure.

Whether recognition of Trump was genuinely necessary to avoid insolvency or was leverage in the naming dispute.

**Contradicted**

The page’s implication that the order contained no discovery provision.

**Unknown**

Whether demolition was genuinely intended, what the full placard said, whether any covered project-scope change has been developed, and how long the immediate safety closure will last.

## Remaining Unknowns

High-value checks still required for a completion-standard audit:

1. Obtain the docket-native September 17 minute entry.
2. Obtain ECF 89’s Exhibits A through D, especially the Delta report, September 13 packet, preliminary September 15 minutes, and Delta addendum.
3. Obtain the current Form 990 and unfinished or completed annual audit directly.
4. Review the September 23 sworn status report when filed.
5. Obtain final approved September 15 minutes and the adopted resolution text.
6. Seek official confirmation or correction of the September 18 layoffs and CFO departure.
7. Determine whether any demolition or replacement plan exists beyond rhetoric, draft material, or speculation.

The repository was not modified. Audit runtime was about 15 minutes.

